/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 38 0 R
/T (6. What is Version Control? Differentiate between Centralized and Distributed VCS. Version Control is a system used to manage and track changes in files, documents, and source code over time. It helps developers maintain project history, recover old versions, and support teamwork in software development. Advantages of Version Control: Tracks file changes Maintains project history Supports collaboration Helps recover previous versions Reduces chances of data loss Centralized Version Control System \(CVCS\) In a centralized version control system, there is one central server that stores all project files and version history. Users connect to the central server to access the project. Features: Single central repository Requires internet/network connection Easier administration Risk of data loss if server fails Example: SVN \(Subversion\) Distributed Version Control System \(DVCS\) In a distributed version control system, every user has a complete copy of the repository along with its history. Users can work offline and later synchronize changes with the remote repository. Features: Complete repository available to every user Faster operations Supports offline work Better backup and security Example: Git Difference: CVCS uses one central repository. DVCS provides a complete repository copy to every user. Git is an example of Distributed VCS.)
/E (6. What is Version Control? Differentiate between Centralized and Distributed VCS. Version Control is a system used to manage and track changes in files, documents, and source code over time. It helps developers maintain project history, recover old versions, and support teamwork in software development. Advantages of Version Control: Tracks file changes Maintains project history Supports collaboration Helps recover previous versions Reduces chances of data loss Centralized Version Control System \(CVCS\) In a centralized version control system, there is one central server that stores all project files and version history. Users connect to the central server to access the project. Features: Single central repository Requires internet/network connection Easier administration Risk of data loss if server fails Example: SVN \(Subversion\) Distributed Version Control System \(DVCS\) In a distributed version control system, every user has a complete copy of the repository along with its history. Users can work offline and later synchronize changes with the remote repository. Features: Complete repository available to every user Faster operations Supports offline work Better backup and security Example: Git Difference: CVCS uses one central repository. DVCS provides a complete repository copy to every user. Git is an example of Distributed VCS.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [0]
>>
